<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Widget styles.

  Only widget-level styling lives here. Colour roles live in colors.xml / values-night/colors.xml,
  text roles in type.xml, and the theme itself in themes.xml.

  Since Version 11 the app is glassmorphic: translucent panes with a hairline rim, over the gradient in
  drawable/bg_glass_gradient.xml. The styles below are where that is defined, so a card looks like a card
  everywhere without any layout repeating the numbers.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
      The app toolbar.

      Chrome, at 97% opacity.

      There WAS a gradient fade at the bottom edge, so content dissolved under it instead of stopping at a
      hard line. It was removed: content scrolls UNDER this bar, and fading it to transparent meant the
      scrolled text stayed legible and collided with the title - two layers of text over each other, which
      is what a user reported as "overlapping" on the Completion screen.

      An app bar is not a card. A card has only the gradient behind it and can be translucent; a bar has
      the whole scrolling app behind it and cannot.
    -->
    <style name="Widget.AlphaRoute.Toolbar" parent="Widget.Material3.Toolbar">
        <item name="android:background">@color/glass_chrome</item>
        <item name="titleTextAppearance">@style/TextAppearance.AlphaRoute.ScreenTitle</item>
        <item name="titleCentered">false</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      A glass card: the Dashboard's stat cards, the History day cards, the Settings panels, the suggestion
      card, the route stop rows.

      Four values make the look, and all four matter:

        cardBackgroundColor  translucent white, so the gradient shows through
        strokeColor          a 1dp hairline LIGHTER than the fill - this is what reads as a glass rim
        strokeWidth          1dp; without it the pane has no edge and reads as a smudge
        cardCornerRadius     24dp. Glass reads as a pane, and panes are not nearly square. Raised from
                             20dp in the first pass: at 20dp the corner was still tight enough to read as
                             a Material card wearing a tint rather than as glass

      cardElevation stays 0dp. A drop shadow under a translucent pane looks wrong - light does not cast a
      hard shadow through frosted glass - and Material's own elevation tint would also grey the fill.
    -->
    <style name="Widget.AlphaRoute.StatCard" parent="Widget.Material3.CardView.Filled">
        <item name="cardCornerRadius">24dp</item>
        <item name="cardElevation">0dp</item>
        <item name="cardBackgroundColor">@color/glass_fill</item>
        <item name="strokeColor">@color/glass_stroke</item>
        <item name="strokeWidth">1dp</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The hero card - the Dashboard's next-stop suggestion.

      A coloured rim instead of the neutral one. That is what separates "the answer to why you opened the
      app" from the seven figures around it, and a rim rather than a fill because the fill still has to be
      glass.

      The radius matches the plain card on purpose. A different radius would read as a different component,
      not as the same component doing a more important job.
    -->
    <style name="Widget.AlphaRoute.StatCard.Hero" parent="Widget.Material3.CardView.Filled">
        <item name="cardCornerRadius">24dp</item>
        <item name="cardElevation">0dp</item>
        <item name="cardBackgroundColor">@color/glass_fill_accent</item>
        <item name="strokeColor">@color/glass_stroke_accent</item>
        <item name="strokeWidth">1dp</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      A glass card that must stand out from the ones around it: the round-complete message and the
      empty-state notice. Same shape and rim, a stronger tinted fill.
    -->
    <style name="Widget.AlphaRoute.StatCard.Accent">
        <item name="cardBackgroundColor">@color/glass_fill_accent</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      ================================================================================================
      A BUTTON INSIDE A BOTTOM SHEET MUST NAME ITS STYLE. THIS IS NOT A PREFERENCE.

      `bottomSheetDialogTheme` is @style/Theme.AlphaRoute.BottomSheet, whose parent chain is

          ThemeOverlay.Material3.BottomSheetDialog
            -> Base.V14.ThemeOverlay.Material3.BottomSheetDialog
              -> Base.ThemeOverlay.Material3.Dialog
                -> materialButtonStyle = Widget.Material3.Button.TextButton.Dialog.Flush

      That last line is Material's own, and it means: **inside any bottom sheet, an unstyled
      MaterialButton is a TEXT BUTTON.** Not filled. A bare `<MaterialButton>` that looks like it will
      be the sheet's primary action renders as plain text with no container at all.

      Five sheets had exactly that - Save, Continue, "Store completed?", "Open Google Maps" and "Start
      navigation" were all meant to be filled and were all text. It reads as a flat, unfinished screen,
      and nothing warns you: the button works, it just does not look like a button.

      So every MaterialButton in a `sheet_*.xml` carries an explicit style:

          style="@style/Widget.Material3.Button"            the primary action, filled
          style="@style/Widget.Material3.Button.TonalButton" a secondary action
          style="@style/Widget.Material3.Button.TextButton"  housekeeping, and the way out

      Verified by reading material-1.14.0.aar on 2026-09-27, not inferred from how the screen looked.
      ================================================================================================
    -->

</resources>
